1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:01,270
In January,

2
00:00:01,570 --> 00:00:03,370
a gallon of regular gas in

3
00:00:03,170 --> 00:00:06,210
America cost about $2.78.

4
00:00:07,137 --> 00:00:07,637
By May,

5
00:00:07,677 --> 00:00:09,117
it hit $4.50.

6
00:00:09,737 --> 00:00:10,377
Today,

7
00:00:10,597 --> 00:00:13,077
it's still around $4.27.

8
00:00:13,915 --> 00:00:17,075
That's an extra $1.49 on every

9
00:00:16,875 --> 00:00:17,695
single gallon.

10
00:00:18,275 --> 00:00:19,335
So where is all that money

11
00:00:19,135 --> 00:00:19,695
going?

12
00:00:20,095 --> 00:00:21,335
And when will it come down?

13
00:00:25,350 --> 00:00:26,870
The story starts on the other

14
00:00:26,670 --> 00:00:27,590
side of the world,

15
00:00:27,910 --> 00:00:29,410
in a narrow channel of water

16
00:00:29,210 --> 00:00:30,883
called the Strait of Hormuz.

17
00:00:31,083 --> 00:00:32,883
It sits between Iran and Oman,

18
00:00:33,303 --> 00:00:34,703
and it's the only way for

19
00:00:34,503 --> 00:00:35,843
ships to sail out of the

20
00:00:35,643 --> 00:00:36,363
Persian Gulf.

21
00:00:36,881 --> 00:00:37,681
Before this year,

22
00:00:37,821 --> 00:00:39,101
about a fifth of all the

23
00:00:38,901 --> 00:00:40,721
oil the world uses passed through

24
00:00:40,521 --> 00:00:41,461
it on tankers,

25
00:00:41,261 --> 00:00:42,801
every single day.

26
00:00:43,211 --> 00:00:44,611
Oil from Saudi Arabia,

27
00:00:44,831 --> 00:00:45,271
Iraq,

28
00:00:45,611 --> 00:00:45,851
Kuwait,

29
00:00:46,151 --> 00:00:48,211
and the United Arab Emirates all

30
00:00:48,011 --> 00:00:49,691
squeezes through this one gap.

31
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:52,090
On February 28,

32
00:00:52,410 --> 00:00:54,490
the United States and Israel launched

33
00:00:54,290 --> 00:00:55,270
strikes on Iran,

34
00:00:55,750 --> 00:00:56,990
and the region went to war.

35
00:00:57,451 --> 00:00:59,391
Iran threatened to attack tankers in

36
00:00:59,191 --> 00:00:59,891
the strait.

37
00:01:00,251 --> 00:01:00,851
Within days,

38
00:01:01,031 --> 00:01:02,491
most shipping through it stopped.

39
00:01:03,014 --> 00:01:04,514
Oil producers like Iraq,

40
00:01:04,954 --> 00:01:05,874
Saudi Arabia,

41
00:01:06,294 --> 00:01:07,974
and the Emirates suddenly had no

42
00:01:07,774 --> 00:01:09,394
way to ship their oil out,

43
00:01:09,534 --> 00:01:10,554
so they cut production.

44
00:01:11,115 --> 00:01:12,015
Almost overnight,

45
00:01:12,335 --> 00:01:14,015
the world lost a huge chunk

46
00:01:13,815 --> 00:01:15,075
of its oil supply,

47
00:01:15,475 --> 00:01:17,095
and there was no spare capacity

48
00:01:16,895 --> 00:01:18,235
left to replace it.

49
00:01:19,383 --> 00:01:20,923
Now watch what happened to the

50
00:01:20,723 --> 00:01:21,503
price of oil.

51
00:01:22,002 --> 00:01:23,102
In early January,

52
00:01:23,482 --> 00:01:25,302
a barrel of American crude cost

53
00:01:25,102 --> 00:01:26,162
about $56.

54
00:01:27,117 --> 00:01:27,777
By April,

55
00:01:27,837 --> 00:01:28,777
it hit $115.

56
00:01:29,677 --> 00:01:30,817
That's more than double,

57
00:01:30,617 --> 00:01:31,977
in about three months.

58
00:01:32,509 --> 00:01:35,669
The US Energy Information Administration says

59
00:01:35,469 --> 00:01:37,549
the jump in global oil prices

60
00:01:37,349 --> 00:01:39,129
in the first three months of

61
00:01:38,929 --> 00:01:40,809
the year was the biggest in

62
00:01:40,609 --> 00:01:42,949
its records going back to 1988,

63
00:01:43,429 --> 00:01:45,469
even after adjusting for inflation.

64
00:01:46,157 --> 00:01:47,897
Prices cooled off over the summer

65
00:01:47,697 --> 00:01:49,877
as some tankers started moving again.

66
00:01:50,577 --> 00:01:51,237
Then this fall,

67
00:01:51,557 --> 00:01:53,137
a new wave of attacks on

68
00:01:52,937 --> 00:01:55,117
ships pushed oil back above $100.

69
00:01:55,937 --> 00:01:58,837
Today it's around $103 a barrel.

70
00:02:00,117 --> 00:02:01,297
So how does a barrel of

71
00:02:01,097 --> 00:02:02,677
oil turn into the number on

72
00:02:02,477 --> 00:02:02,937
the pump?

73
00:02:03,418 --> 00:02:05,338
One barrel holds 42 gallons.

74
00:02:06,058 --> 00:02:08,078
At about $103 a barrel,

75
00:02:08,398 --> 00:02:10,338
the crude oil alone costs about

76
00:02:10,138 --> 00:02:13,798
$2.46 for every gallon of gas.

77
00:02:14,335 --> 00:02:18,155
That's about 58% of the $4.27

78
00:02:17,955 --> 00:02:19,035
you pay at the pump.

79
00:02:19,493 --> 00:02:21,193
The rest pays for refining the

80
00:02:20,993 --> 00:02:21,953
crude into gasoline,

81
00:02:22,573 --> 00:02:23,693
trucking it to your town,

82
00:02:23,953 --> 00:02:25,273
running the gas station,

83
00:02:25,333 --> 00:02:25,833
and taxes.

84
00:02:26,527 --> 00:02:29,287
The federal gas tax is 18.4

85
00:02:29,087 --> 00:02:29,827
cents a gallon,

86
00:02:30,267 --> 00:02:32,407
and it hasn't changed since 1993.

87
00:02:33,227 --> 00:02:34,927
States add their own tax on

88
00:02:34,727 --> 00:02:35,207
top,

89
00:02:35,447 --> 00:02:37,087
which is why prices are so

90
00:02:36,887 --> 00:02:38,487
different from state to state.

91
00:02:38,981 --> 00:02:40,401
But because crude is the biggest

92
00:02:40,201 --> 00:02:40,681
piece,

93
00:02:40,961 --> 00:02:41,981
when oil goes up,

94
00:02:41,981 --> 00:02:42,961
your gas goes up.

95
00:02:43,261 --> 00:02:44,621
There's no way around it.

96
00:02:45,750 --> 00:02:47,630
Here's the part that confuses a

97
00:02:47,430 --> 00:02:48,230
lot of people.

98
00:02:48,730 --> 00:02:50,430
The United States is the biggest

99
00:02:50,230 --> 00:02:51,890
oil producer in the world.

100
00:02:52,337 --> 00:02:53,697
So why should a war on

101
00:02:53,497 --> 00:02:54,717
the other side of the planet

102
00:02:54,517 --> 00:02:56,457
raise prices here at home?

103
00:02:56,897 --> 00:02:58,737
Because oil is sold on one

104
00:02:58,537 --> 00:02:59,657
global market.

105
00:03:00,217 --> 00:03:01,597
If a buyer in Asia will

106
00:03:01,397 --> 00:03:03,337
pay $110 a barrel,

107
00:03:03,777 --> 00:03:05,697
a Texas oil company won't sell

108
00:03:05,497 --> 00:03:06,757
it to you for $60.

109
00:03:07,622 --> 00:03:09,322
When supply disappears anywhere,

110
00:03:09,642 --> 00:03:10,922
the price goes up everywhere,

111
00:03:11,262 --> 00:03:13,002
including at the gas station down

112
00:03:12,802 --> 00:03:13,422
your street.

113
00:03:13,889 --> 00:03:15,409
Producing a lot of our own

114
00:03:15,209 --> 00:03:16,249
oil does help.

115
00:03:16,729 --> 00:03:18,949
It keeps supplies flowing and softens

116
00:03:18,749 --> 00:03:19,289
the blow.

117
00:03:19,749 --> 00:03:21,409
It just can't make prices here

118
00:03:21,209 --> 00:03:22,529
immune to the rest of the

119
00:03:22,329 --> 00:03:22,729
world.

120
00:03:23,950 --> 00:03:25,230
Oil shocks aren't new.

121
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:27,130
Here's the price of gas since

122
00:03:26,930 --> 00:03:27,790
1990,

123
00:03:28,010 --> 00:03:29,390
adjusted for inflation.

124
00:03:30,030 --> 00:03:30,850
In 1990,

125
00:03:31,150 --> 00:03:32,390
Iraq invaded Kuwait,

126
00:03:32,190 --> 00:03:33,610
and gas jumped.

127
00:03:34,206 --> 00:03:35,306
In 2008,

128
00:03:35,406 --> 00:03:37,386
oil hit record highs during a

129
00:03:37,186 --> 00:03:37,986
global boom,

130
00:03:38,306 --> 00:03:40,126
right before the financial crisis.

131
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:41,870
In 2022,

132
00:03:42,070 --> 00:03:43,570
Russia invaded Ukraine,

133
00:03:43,370 --> 00:03:46,030
and gas topped $5 a gallon

134
00:03:45,830 --> 00:03:46,990
for the first time.

135
00:03:47,422 --> 00:03:47,962
And now,

136
00:03:47,882 --> 00:03:49,002
in 2026,

137
00:03:49,342 --> 00:03:50,282
the war with Iran.

138
00:03:51,022 --> 00:03:52,562
Every big spike on this chart

139
00:03:52,362 --> 00:03:53,962
started with the same thing:

140
00:03:54,282 --> 00:03:56,162
oil supply that suddenly looked unsafe.

141
00:03:57,650 --> 00:03:59,610
You've probably noticed something else that

142
00:03:59,410 --> 00:04:00,270
feels unfair.

143
00:04:00,850 --> 00:04:01,930
Gas goes up fast,

144
00:04:02,150 --> 00:04:03,350
but comes down slowly.

145
00:04:03,917 --> 00:04:05,557
From January to the spring peak,

146
00:04:05,837 --> 00:04:09,217
oil rose by about $1.39 for

147
00:04:09,017 --> 00:04:10,597
every gallon's worth of crude.

148
00:04:11,257 --> 00:04:13,097
Pump prices rose even more,

149
00:04:13,377 --> 00:04:15,257
about $1.72.

150
00:04:16,050 --> 00:04:16,470
Then,

151
00:04:16,490 --> 00:04:17,690
from April to July,

152
00:04:17,830 --> 00:04:20,890
oil fell by about $1.07 a

153
00:04:20,690 --> 00:04:21,110
gallon.

154
00:04:21,750 --> 00:04:23,670
But pump prices only fell about

155
00:04:23,470 --> 00:04:24,310
$0.72.

156
00:04:25,133 --> 00:04:26,813
Economists have a name for this:

157
00:04:27,393 --> 00:04:28,153
rockets and feathers.

158
00:04:28,773 --> 00:04:30,393
Prices shoot up like a rocket,

159
00:04:30,193 --> 00:04:31,913
and float down like a feather.

160
00:04:32,381 --> 00:04:34,781
One explanation is that stations raise

161
00:04:34,581 --> 00:04:36,701
prices fast to cover their next,

162
00:04:37,221 --> 00:04:38,521
more expensive delivery,

163
00:04:39,041 --> 00:04:40,021
but cut them slowly,

164
00:04:40,461 --> 00:04:42,461
because drivers don't shop around as

165
00:04:42,261 --> 00:04:43,941
hard when prices are falling.

166
00:04:45,217 --> 00:04:46,777
And gas isn't the only fuel.

167
00:04:47,597 --> 00:04:48,617
Diesel powers the trucks,

168
00:04:48,837 --> 00:04:49,277
trains,

169
00:04:49,617 --> 00:04:50,257
tractors,

170
00:04:50,057 --> 00:04:52,037
and ships that move almost everything

171
00:04:51,837 --> 00:04:52,517
we buy.

172
00:04:53,019 --> 00:04:53,619
This year,

173
00:04:53,659 --> 00:04:56,779
diesel went from about $3.46 a

174
00:04:56,579 --> 00:04:58,059
gallon to over $6.

175
00:04:58,619 --> 00:05:01,759
Today it's around $6.35.

176
00:05:02,635 --> 00:05:04,755
Diesel jumped even more than gasoline,

177
00:05:05,495 --> 00:05:07,335
because the Middle East also exports

178
00:05:07,135 --> 00:05:09,015
a lot of diesel and jet

179
00:05:08,815 --> 00:05:09,295
fuel,

180
00:05:09,595 --> 00:05:11,135
and those shipments were cut off

181
00:05:11,075 --> 00:05:11,375
too.

182
00:05:11,825 --> 00:05:13,025
When it costs more to move

183
00:05:12,825 --> 00:05:13,645
food and goods,

184
00:05:14,085 --> 00:05:15,845
those costs slowly show up on

185
00:05:15,645 --> 00:05:16,485
store shelves,

186
00:05:16,525 --> 00:05:16,845
too.

187
00:05:17,323 --> 00:05:19,183
That's why energy prices are up

188
00:05:18,983 --> 00:05:20,863
about 20% from a year ago,

189
00:05:21,323 --> 00:05:23,603
and overall inflation climbed from about

190
00:05:23,403 --> 00:05:28,203
2.4% in February to 3.7% in

191
00:05:28,003 --> 00:05:28,463
September.

192
00:05:29,850 --> 00:05:30,970
So what does all of this

193
00:05:30,770 --> 00:05:31,750
mean for your wallet?

194
00:05:32,362 --> 00:05:33,702
Say you fill up a 15

195
00:05:33,502 --> 00:05:34,942
gallon tank once a week.

196
00:05:35,482 --> 00:05:38,582
At $1.49 more per gallon than

197
00:05:38,382 --> 00:05:39,062
in January,

198
00:05:39,382 --> 00:05:41,982
that's about $22 more every single

199
00:05:41,782 --> 00:05:42,322
week.

200
00:05:42,789 --> 00:05:43,529
Over a year,

201
00:05:43,629 --> 00:05:45,929
that adds up to about $1,160.

202
00:05:47,029 --> 00:05:48,389
It's like a new bill that

203
00:05:48,189 --> 00:05:49,769
nobody asked you to sign up

204
00:05:49,569 --> 00:05:50,049
for.

205
00:05:50,442 --> 00:05:51,682
And if you drive for work,

206
00:05:51,482 --> 00:05:53,362
or your family has two cars,

207
00:05:53,662 --> 00:05:55,242
it can easily be double that.

208
00:05:56,450 --> 00:05:57,530
Does it feel like the most

209
00:05:57,330 --> 00:05:58,790
expensive gas ever?

210
00:05:59,170 --> 00:05:59,810
It isn't,

211
00:05:59,690 --> 00:06:00,610
at least not yet.

212
00:06:01,095 --> 00:06:02,535
In June 2022,

213
00:06:02,855 --> 00:06:04,755
the national average peaked at about

214
00:06:04,555 --> 00:06:07,135
$5.01 a gallon.

215
00:06:07,618 --> 00:06:09,298
And in the summer of 2008,

216
00:06:09,498 --> 00:06:11,098
after adjusting for inflation,

217
00:06:11,498 --> 00:06:15,558
gas cost about $6.33 in today's

218
00:06:15,358 --> 00:06:15,818
money.

219
00:06:16,231 --> 00:06:17,671
So today's prices hurt,

220
00:06:17,911 --> 00:06:19,011
but we have been here before,

221
00:06:19,591 --> 00:06:21,271
and both of those spikes eventually

222
00:06:21,331 --> 00:06:22,191
came back down.

223
00:06:23,483 --> 00:06:24,823
So when will gas get cheaper?

224
00:06:25,643 --> 00:06:25,843
Honestly,

225
00:06:26,063 --> 00:06:27,343
nobody knows for sure.

226
00:06:27,823 --> 00:06:29,363
It mostly depends on one thing:

227
00:06:29,503 --> 00:06:31,843
ships moving safely through Hormuz again.

228
00:06:32,374 --> 00:06:33,894
If tankers can pass safely,

229
00:06:34,114 --> 00:06:35,274
oil could fall quickly,

230
00:06:35,794 --> 00:06:37,234
and pump prices would follow,

231
00:06:37,034 --> 00:06:37,694
slowly,

232
00:06:37,494 --> 00:06:38,534
like a feather.

233
00:06:39,025 --> 00:06:40,305
If the attacks continue,

234
00:06:40,565 --> 00:06:42,345
prices could stay high or climb

235
00:06:42,145 --> 00:06:43,025
even further.

236
00:06:43,563 --> 00:06:45,983
The US keeps an emergency stockpile

237
00:06:45,783 --> 00:06:48,183
called the Strategic Petroleum Reserve,

238
00:06:48,563 --> 00:06:51,483
stored in giant underground salt caverns

239
00:06:51,283 --> 00:06:52,643
along the Gulf Coast.

240
00:06:53,158 --> 00:06:54,798
Releasing some of it adds supply

241
00:06:54,598 --> 00:06:56,538
for a while and can calm

242
00:06:56,338 --> 00:06:56,898
prices.

243
00:06:57,598 --> 00:06:59,198
But no reserve can replace a

244
00:06:58,998 --> 00:07:00,518
fifth of the world's oil for

245
00:07:00,318 --> 00:07:00,718
long.

246
00:07:01,278 --> 00:07:01,878
It's a patch,

247
00:07:02,058 --> 00:07:02,778
not a fix.

248
00:07:04,083 --> 00:07:04,783
In the meantime,

249
00:07:05,063 --> 00:07:06,983
a few simple things genuinely help.

250
00:07:07,470 --> 00:07:09,050
Use a gas price app to

251
00:07:08,850 --> 00:07:10,130
compare nearby stations.

252
00:07:10,730 --> 00:07:12,590
Prices just a mile apart can

253
00:07:12,390 --> 00:07:13,150
differ a lot.

254
00:07:13,609 --> 00:07:15,409
Keep your tires properly inflated,

255
00:07:15,649 --> 00:07:16,389
drive smoothly,

256
00:07:16,709 --> 00:07:18,029
and combine short trips.

257
00:07:18,609 --> 00:07:20,649
Hard braking and fast starts burn

258
00:07:20,449 --> 00:07:21,409
extra fuel.

259
00:07:21,923 --> 00:07:24,283
Warehouse clubs and grocery store reward

260
00:07:24,083 --> 00:07:26,363
programs often knock a few cents

261
00:07:26,163 --> 00:07:27,263
off every gallon,

262
00:07:27,263 --> 00:07:27,643
too.

263
00:07:28,105 --> 00:07:29,105
And if you pay at the

264
00:07:28,905 --> 00:07:30,125
pump with a credit card,

265
00:07:30,445 --> 00:07:31,505
try to pay it off in

266
00:07:31,305 --> 00:07:31,805
full,

267
00:07:31,605 --> 00:07:33,845
because the next problem is interest

268
00:07:33,645 --> 00:07:34,205
rates.

269
00:07:35,483 --> 00:07:37,383
So gas didn't get expensive by

270
00:07:37,183 --> 00:07:37,723
accident.

271
00:07:38,443 --> 00:07:39,903
A war shut the world's most

272
00:07:39,703 --> 00:07:41,163
important oil route,

273
00:07:41,343 --> 00:07:42,003
oil doubled,

274
00:07:42,323 --> 00:07:44,023
and the cost flowed straight into

275
00:07:43,823 --> 00:07:44,383
your tank.

276
00:07:44,907 --> 00:07:47,487
Higher fuel prices pushed inflation up

277
00:07:47,287 --> 00:07:49,587
so much that the Federal Reserve

278
00:07:49,387 --> 00:07:51,527
raised interest rates for the first

279
00:07:51,327 --> 00:07:53,067
time since 2023.

280
00:07:53,478 --> 00:07:54,178
Next time:

281
00:07:54,338 --> 00:07:55,878
what that rate hike means for

282
00:07:55,678 --> 00:07:56,538
your credit cards,

283
00:07:56,858 --> 00:07:57,458
your savings,

284
00:07:57,738 --> 00:07:58,538
and your mortgage.

